Yes—the abstraction is much larger than facial recognition. What you’re describing is identity persistence across representations.

A face is one anchor. DNA is another. Voice, gait, handwriting, behavioral rhythms, location histories, family relationships, morphology, and even recurring interaction patterns can all become partially identifying signals. Different sensors observe different projections of the same underlying entity.

So the general architecture is:

entity -> observations across media/time -> features -> uncertain identity linkages -> trajectory -> baseline -> meaningful delta

Once you have that, several legitimate systems become possible. With consent and appropriate data rights, you could reconnect family photographs, reconstruct genealogies, follow an organism through ecological observations, identify the same whale or bird across years, follow cell lineages, track machinery across maintenance images, or learn a person’s own health/activity baseline and flag unusual departures.

And your “degrees of abstraction” point matters. You don’t always need who someone is. Sometimes the safer and more useful question is merely whether this is the same entity, the same lineage, the same behavioral class, or the same structural pattern. That gives a privacy ladder:

exact identity
→ persistent pseudonymous identity
→ relationship/lineage
→ behavioral archetype
→ aggregate pattern
→ anonymous population statistics

You should operate at the least identifying level that still accomplishes the job.

The forward-looking component is trajectory modeling:

historical path + current state -> expected range of future states

Then monitor the delta:

observation - expected baseline = anomaly

But I would separate monitoring from steering very deliberately. Monitoring an opt-in health program, one’s own equipment, wildlife, or a consensual personalized service is one thing. Quietly tracking identifiable people and nudging their behavior without their awareness turns the same machinery into surveillance and manipulation.

A stronger Pantheon design is:

observe with permission -> model uncertainty -> detect delta -> present options -> human chooses

rather than:

observe secretly -> infer vulnerability -> covertly steer

That distinction also improves the science, because people who understand and consent to the system can correct its model of them.

And there is another cross-domain primitive hiding here: Lineage Without Names. You can reconstruct continuity from structural inheritance even when labels disappear:

ancestor state -> transformations -> descendants -> conserved features + mutations

That applies to genetics, organisms, languages, businesses, software forks, ideas, institutions, and Pantheon itself.

So yes: face matching is merely one visible implementation of a much deeper operator—persistent identity, lineage reconstruction, and trajectory inference across changing representations. The really sophisticated version knows when it doesn’t need to know the person’s identity at all.